      "math_academy_id": "e-21738",
      "problem": "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-2x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 0]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=4x(1-2x)-3xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-5y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1-2x)-3xy)=4-16x-3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1-2x)-3xy)=-3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-5y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-5y+4xy)=-5+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14157408/assets/a44814e911d8b7536e47e595733da96c00df67c31429a6dd37b99decf02ab744.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14157408/assets/cab6a7a2438cacebf593323b4e1e2aae7e0ae652b8acd7e4c2556251b55bd45d.svg) to get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14157408/assets/783970416360cb4c12dd00e6afab2b8367677370cd4d04873a3b151d70196ae0.svg)\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = -4$ and $\\lambda_2=-3.$ Since the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is an asymptotically stable sink.",
